\begin{remark}[Probit regularizer and GELU]\label{rem:probit_gelu}
The framework is not tied to the entropy. For the probit regularizer $\Omega_\Phi(\pi) \coloneqq -\phi\bigl(\Phi^{-1}(\pi)\bigr)$, with $\phi, \Phi$ the standard Gaussian density and distribution function, one has $\frac{\diff}{\diff \pi}\bigl(-\Omega_\Phi\bigr)(\pi) = -\Phi^{-1}(\pi)$, so the first-order condition for~\eqref{eq:regularized_gate} gives the gate $\pi_{\Omega_\Phi}(\Delta) = \Phi(\Delta)$ and the conjugate activation
\begin{equation*}
	\Omega_\Phi^*(\Delta) = \Delta\, \Phi(\Delta) + \phi(\Delta) = \E_{\varepsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)}\bigl[ \max(\Delta + \varepsilon, 0) \bigr],
\end{equation*}
the Gaussian smoothing of the \ac{relu}. This differs from GELU $\Delta\Phi(\Delta)$ \parencite{Hendrycks2016} by the term $\phi(\Delta)$, and the gate $\Phi(\Delta) = \E_\varepsilon\bigl[\1[\Delta + \varepsilon > 0]\bigr]$ is the hard stopping gate averaged over a Gaussian shift of its threshold---matching the original stochastic-gate motivation for GELU. The distinction matters for exact decomposability: the conjugate activation $\Omega_\Phi^*(\Delta) = \Delta\,\Phi(\Delta) + \phi(\Delta)$ is the object recovered by $\freg$ and, being a Fenchel conjugate, is convex, non-decreasing, and $1$-Lipschitz (Theorem~\ref{thm:freg_validity}). GELU $\Delta \mapsto \Delta\,\Phi(\Delta)$ is non-monotone---it dips below zero for $\Delta < 0$---and is therefore \emph{not} itself a conjugate activation $\Omega^*$; the exactly decomposable object is the $+\phi(\Delta)$ variant, and networks using GELU are only approximately captured by this decomposition.
\end{remark}

